--- CHECK 2: trim markers changed only whitespace — CONFIRMED, with one caveat ---
Diffed the four pre-existing kinds' rendered bytes (income/expenses/
savings/savings-rate) between master's template and attempt 3's template
(same Go binary/fixture, template swapped): the ONLY diff on all four is a
single new whitespace-only line before the first `<tr>`
(`60a61 >                     ` / `61a62 >                     `),
introduced by the new `{{$noCoverage := and (eq .Type "healthcare")
.HealthcareNoCoverageInRange}}` assignment line, which has no `{{- -}}`
trim markers and so leaves its own newline+indentation in the output —
unconditionally, for every kind, not just healthcare. This is a text node
containing only whitespace: no new element, no attribute change, no
content change, and browsers/AT collapse/ignore whitespace-only text
nodes, so it is INERT to accessibility (no a11y-tree node, no visual
change — confirmed the `<tr>` immediately follows in all rendered
screenshots-equivalent structure). Per this task's specific ask ("confirm
the trim markers changed only whitespace, no content/structure change") —
CONFIRMED, this is whitespace-only. OBSERVATION (not an a11y FAIL, flagged
for checker-tests): the KD1.3 manifest's claim that the false-path output
is now "byte-identical to what a single unconditional line would have
produced — matching the pre-KD1 baseline exactly" is factually incorrect
by one blank line; K5 (byte-identical pre-existing-kind responses) is a
checker-tests criterion, not mine, but the provenance is worth recording
since the manifest asserts a stronger claim than the bytes support.
The two `{{if}}/{{else}}/{{end}}` wrapper blocks themselves (Per-Month
tile, vs-Avg cell) DO trim correctly: false-branch output for all four
kinds is otherwise byte-identical to master (confirmed above), matching
attempt 2's already-PASSed structure/heading findings.

--- CHECK 3: refund-dominant month — sign conveyed by text, not color alone ---
Rendered living-refund.html (Jan nets +500 refund -> Value = -500; Feb
nets -1000 spend -> Value = +1000, per ruling KD-2026-08-30d's negated-
signed-sum contract):
  Jan: <td class="p-3 text-sm text-right text-red-600 dark:text-red-400">-$500.00</td>
  Feb: <td class="p-3 text-sm text-right text-red-600 dark:text-red-400">$1,000.00</td>
Both cells use the IDENTICAL unconditional class string
(text-red-600 dark:text-red-400) regardless of sign — formatMoney (Go,
internal/templates/render.go:425-453, confirmed by reading the source)
prefixes negatives with a literal "-$" (not a suppressed/CSS-only minus),
so the negative/positive distinction is carried by the RENDERED TEXT
("-$500.00" vs "$1,000.00"), satisfying point 8 (no color-only signaling)
and point 13 (sign semantics in accessible text — the minus sign IS the
accessible text here, read aloud by any screen reader as "hyphen 500
dollars" or similar, not hidden). Since both signs render under the same
class, "the negative value's contrast in both themes is unchanged from
the positive case" per the check requirement — CONFIRMED, same classes,
same computed contrast (light text-red-600 on bg-white / dark
text-red-400 on bg-gray-800, and on hover — this exact pairing is
pre-existing/byte-identical to master's Value-cell class shape, already
logged as an OBSERVATION in the attempt-2 verdict at 3.73:1 dark-hover;
unchanged by attempt 3, out of KD1's scope, not re-triggered here since no
new class was introduced for this cell).
The vs-Avg cell for the refund row also renders as text, not color alone:
Jan -293.8% (text-green-700/dark:text-green-400, since -500 <= avg),
Feb +287.7% (text-red-600/dark:text-red-400, literal "+" via &#43;) — the
sign is always in the glyph, color is decorative. This is pre-existing
percentDiff/formatPercent behavior (same class pattern already used by
the income kind), not new to attempt 3.

